selected_assets.prompt:
Use case: compositing
Asset type: SECOND connected cinematic still, driver-eye viewpoint after camera enters the incoming Explorer through its lowered driver-side window. One coherent photograph, not a collage, UI, panel or animation.
REFERENCE ROLES: Input image 1 is the SELECTED BASEMENT MASTER and locks the exact room, parking arrangement, cars, lighting, materials and scene continuity. Inputs 2–5 are strict eight-angle identity sheets: corrected old Lancer, Cruze, dark boxy Cherokee KK, and approved 2011-era Explorer. The burgundy Camry is locked to its selected master depiction, which was generated from its own eight-angle sheet. Use these established sheet geometries, not generic model substitutions.
CHANGE ONLY THE CAMERA LOCATION AND ADD ITS CABIN VIEW: camera has moved into the SAME incoming SILVER 2011-era Explorer from the master, at a realistic driver's eye position on the left. The Explorer has just stopped at the foot of the descending basement ramp. Look forward through its large clear windshield toward the SAME seven parked vehicles. The incoming silver Explorer is now the camera's own cabin, so its exterior is not visible as another car parked ahead.
CABIN: believable inferred 2011-era Ford Explorer interior, restrained dark charcoal dashboard, analog instrument gauges, traditional left steering wheel with a small Ford oval badge and physical buttons, conventional physical climate/audio controls and at most a small unlit factory center screen. NO giant touchscreen, futuristic digital cockpit, HUD, glowing graphics, racing wheel or luxury redesign. This is a concept cabin, not an exact photographed real unit. Cabin/dashboard/partially cropped steering wheel occupy ONLY approximately the lower 25% of the frame, plus slender edge A-pillars and a very thin upper roof edge. Keep the steering wheel low enough not to hide any parked car. The driver's side window at the left edge is OPEN/LOWERED with no glass pane across the opening, suggesting where the camera entered. No driver, hands, people, visible drone or camera equipment. Clear realistic windshield glass with only faint reflections that do not obscure vehicles.
EXACT SEVEN PARKED VEHICLES VISIBLE THROUGH THE WINDSHIELD, all fronts and whole individual silhouettes readable, physically separated with no occlusion, unchanged order left-to-right from the master:
1 taupe corrected old Lancer;
2 black first-generation Cruze;
3 burgundy Camry;
4 dark black/navy boxy Cherokee KK with upright rectangular lights/chrome seven-slot grille;
5 WHITE 2011-era Explorer with approved three-bar front grille and body;
6 SILVER corrected old Lancer;
7 DARK BLUE corrected old Lancer.
All three Lancers share exactly the corrected reference sheet's narrow swept clear headlamps, slim upper grille, rounded body and small factory wheels; only their paint differs. The white parked Explorer has exactly the approved 2011-era body, and is the ONLY complete Explorer seen ahead. Do not add a parked silver Explorer, duplicate any other car, omit any car, merge vehicles or shuffle the order.
SPATIAL CONTINUITY: keep the same low concrete basement ceiling and beams, utility pipes, concrete columns at perimeter, ceiling LED arrangement, white walls/navy lower band/thin lime accent, service lifts and red/dark tool cabinets, and gray floor from image 1. Preserve the exact seven parking locations and their orientations; only perspective changes naturally because the camera moved a few meters into the existing Explorer. Keep a clear center aisle and balanced neutral light with gentle warm light from the ramp BEHIND the cabin. NO aboveground windows, sky, sunlight streaming from side windows, warehouse trusses or new architecture.
COMPOSITION: same 16:9 aspect as master, high-resolution cinematic photoreal automotive photography, wide but plausible interior lens with straight architecture. Most of the frame is the windshield view of the seven cars and basement, with a low band of tactile cabin foreground establishing first-person perspective. No overlays, typography, captions, signs, brand wall graphics, interface elements, watermarks or decorative logos. Ordinary vehicle emblems only. Generate exactly ONE connected interior still.
